From 64450cd5b172ad6aa1e7aaf2ddc802dcddcfd861 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pavanteja295 <pavanteja295@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 10:39:49 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] Rasterise the UV maps from the head's triangles only

MetaHuman authors one UV chart per material and every chart fills the whole 0-1
tile, because in a normal renderer each material samples its own texture and
overlap costs nothing. This code rasterises ONE shared map over every triangle it
is given, so an appended mesh lands on top of the head's chart.

Measured at uv_size 256 with the eyeballs appended: the head fills 62,362 texels
on its own, and 627 of them -- 1.0% of the head's map -- are taken by eyeball
geometry, where the position written is wrong by up to 730 mm. Contested is not
the same as corrupted: the rasteriser resolves each contested texel and picks the
head in 98.7% of them.

Repacking the atlas fixes this and costs far more than it is worth here. It moves
the head's own UVs, so the head stops being bit-identical to the existing asset,
every region mask needs rebaking, and runs stop being comparable.

None of that is needed, because a mesh the rig alone drives has nothing to look
up. It takes no network offset, so it needs no presence in the map. Leaving it
out is the whole fix.

  position map and displacement map with the eyeballs appended are now
  bit-identical to the head-only asset's, 0.000e+00 over 196,608 values

The read side was already covered: freeze_rig_only forces every eyeball blob's
offset back to identity after the network runs, so it does not matter which
texel one of them samples. This closes the write side, which it did not.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
