From 49cfc46fcd089438115ed041c9fc7bc922d53151 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pavanteja295 <pavanteja295@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 11:45:48 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] Hold rig-only blobs on the surface they belong to

An eyeball is a rigid sphere whose geometry we already know exactly. Blobs start
at their triangle centres, which IS the ball's surface, so there is nothing about
where they sit for the optimiser to discover -- only what they look like.

Left free they leave. Measured on G1 at iteration 33,000:

  on or inside the ball   61% of eyeball blobs   median opacity 0.03
  15-25 mm out             36%                   median opacity 0.90
  loose, past 25 mm         3%                   median opacity 0.91

The optimiser evacuated the ball: everything still on it is switched off, and
everything that left is opaque. The cause is that threshold_xyz is an ABSOLUTE
10 mm, calibrated for a head 350 mm tall. Applied unchanged to a 26 mm eyeball,
the leash is longer than the object, so a blob can leave the surface entirely
without paying anything. The eyeballs are not drifting unusually -- head blobs
drift further (7.86 mm median against 7.16 mm) -- they are simply an order of
magnitude smaller than the thing the rule was written for.

Two ways out, both here. threshold_xyz_rig shortens the leash to 1 mm for
rig-only meshes. pin_rig_only, the default, drops their position gradient
altogether, which is the honest statement of the situation: the geometry is
known, so only colour, opacity and scale are fitted.

This is also what makes gaze possible later. A blob pinned to the ball rotates
with it correctly; one sitting 10 mm off sweeps an exaggerated arc.

Both are no-ops on an asset with no rig-only mesh, so head-only runs are
unchanged and stay comparable.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
