From 49c555904a935a5ec4c5603d0f68e3ba6cc0cbf4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pavanteja295 <pavanteja295@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 10:23:10 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 6/8] Silencing the skin over the eye does not make the eyeball
 necessary

The head mesh is sealed across the eye: 1,663 triangles of skin span the aperture
and are what currently draws his eyes. The hypothesis was that an eyeball added
behind that surface has nothing to do -- measured on G2, 57% of eyeball blobs sit
on the ball at opacity 0.042 -- and that silencing the skin from the start of
training would force the ball to learn the iris instead.

It does not. blind_aperture renders those blobs at zero opacity; nothing is
deleted, so no index moves and the flag is reversible.

  G2  ball surface 57.1%, opacity 0.042    PSNR 21.8752  LPIPS 0.1085
  G3  ball surface 55.2%, opacity 0.043    PSNR 21.9105  LPIPS 0.1055

The ball's opacity moved by 0.001 over a full six epochs. The metrics improved
slightly, which is what removing 1,663 blobs and letting densification spend them
elsewhere would do on its own, and is not evidence for the mechanism.

WHY IT FAILED, which is the part worth keeping

Blobs are silenced by BINDING, and a blob sits up to ~7 mm from the triangle it is
bound to. So the aperture never emptied -- it refilled from the neighbours. In G3,
339 visible blobs sit within 3 mm of the silenced skin and EVERY ONE of them is
bound elsewhere and drifted in. In G2 the same region held 302, of which 207 had
drifted in.

Constraining a region by binding does not constrain it in space. Making the
eyeball necessary would mean constraining drift as well, which is a deeper change
than this experiment was worth.

aperture_triangles.npz is a property of the topology, not of a person: identical
between drk and the MetaHuman archetype, 1,663 = 1,663 with zero differences.

