From 5299964d0063e8b786a1b32a195adb1a64fdfc4d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pavanteja295 <pavanteja295@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 21:54:04 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] Train on the crops at their own size, and hold LPIPS
 where it was

Three changes, all of which are no-ops on a 512 corpus.

A corpus stored at native resolution has a different image size per chunk, and the
evaluation loop stacked sixteen frames into one tensor to score LPIPS. That raises on
the first pair that disagrees. It now scores one frame at a time; the term is summed and
divided by the frame count either way, so it is the same number, not an approximation.

The LPIPS term is held at a fixed 512 side, in the loss and in the evaluation. VGG
forward and backward peaks at 0.86 GiB at 512 and 7.86 GiB at 1600, measured on this
card, and the term switches on at half the iterations -- so without this a native run
trains for hours and then runs out of memory. The second reason is the one that decided
it: VGG has a fixed receptive field, so on a 1600px face the same network measures
similarity over a third of the area it covered at 512, and lambda_lpips 0.05 was tuned
against the 512 behaviour. Holding it keeps that term as G4 had it and lets L1 and SSIM
take the extra detail, which is a smaller departure from G4 than letting it drift.

densify_grad_threshold is an absolute constant compared against a screen-space gradient
that comes from a loss averaged over pixels, so the same number is not the same policy
at a new size. Rather than argue which way it moves, the distribution is logged. Measured
over two short runs: the fraction of blobs over 0.0002 is 3.99% at 512 and 2.89% at
native. The error-based clone threshold, which the same reasoning predicted would
collapse because its statistic is divided by pixels_per_gs, does not move at all --
0.160 against 0.170. That is why it is logged and not reasoned about.

